WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:01.589 align:start position:0%
 
he  bought a knife walked into the salon  where holly was working as a hairdresser  and stabbed her 14 times  holly gazard was just 20 years old when  her ex-boyfriend murdered her that was  in 2014. since then many more women have  been murdered and her father says not  enough progress has been made  when i read the sentencing of sarah  everard and i read the  victim impact statements from her mum  and dad and her sister it took me right  back to when we had those victim impact  statements in the court we've not come a  long way in that sense you know i'm  still hearing the same things as we said  when holly was killed  if women were already frightened to walk  the streets at night the recent deaths  of sarah everard and sabine nessa have  reinforced those fears and sparked a  conversation about how to protect women  and telecoms company bt have come up  with one possible solution  the walk me home service which is  currently in development would allow  users to opt in to a gps tracking  service it will be activated by calling  888 before embarking on a journey if the  user doesn't reach their destination on  time it will trigger an alert and  emergency contacts and the police will  be called  latest figures show that in the last  decade  1425 women were killed by a man that's  around one every three days but when  sarah everard was walking through a  clapham common she was on the phone to  her boyfriend and sabine anessa was  killed just meters away from her home  suggesting tracking women may not be the  solution  and the women of clapham common had  mixed reactions  i think it's good to have the option  there to use it um but again completely  agree  there's a massive educational piece that  needs to be done if it reassures you as  a person a man or woman then i think  it's a great thing i don't actually like  the idea of having my data trapped and  people having like tabs on me all the  time i'll probably stick to sharing my  whatsapp location with family and  friends  this app has been backed by the home  secretary and is aiming to launch ahead  of christmas but there are already apps  out there including one set up by nick  which has hundreds of thousands of users  and he says these methods may only work  in the short term  this is a bigger  bigger fish than just providing an app  or a phone line to help keep people safe  it's a society issue  but change in the culture of society  doesn't occur overnight this app may not  address all the dangers facing women and  girls but could be one way that some  women feel safer on the streets  shimane freeman powell sky news

